## Summary

Add a Split Workspace system that allows multiple normal Ephervex host workspaces to be visible at the same time.

The goal is to make it possible to work with several views of one or more installed clients/local servers without turning Ephervex into a general-purpose IDE docking system.

A pane chooses where a host and one of its surfaces are displayed. It must not become the owner of the underlying host or feature state.

## Supported layouts

The first implementation should support the constrained layout set:

- `1:1`
- `2:1`
- `1:2`
- `2:2`

The layout should be represented explicitly in the application model rather than inferred from arbitrary UI geometry.

Closing/splitting panes should always normalize into one of the supported layouts deterministically.

## Pane behavior

Each pane should independently select:

- an installed client or local server;
- one surface/tool valid for that host.

Examples that should be possible:

- client Mods + client Logs;
- client Diagnostics + client Overview;
- client Isolation + client Logs;
- client in one pane + local server in another;
- the same client displayed in multiple panes with different surfaces.

The same host appearing in two panes must be valid.

Changing the selected surface in one pane must not change the selected surface in another pane.

## Shared host state

Displaying the same host more than once must not duplicate its underlying state.

State belonging to the host or a feature remains shared, including things such as:

- running state;
- restart progress;
- host mutation/busy state;
- Isolation workflow state;
- Diagnostics state;
- snapshot operations;
- mod operations;
- server lifecycle state.

For example, if a client is restarting from pane A, pane B displaying the same client should observe the same restart state and must not be able to launch another conflicting restart.

A pane owns presentation selection only.

## Architecture

Reuse the existing `HostWorkspace` rather than introducing split-specific client/server workspace implementations.

The split parent should primarily own:

- pane composition;
- pane ordering;
- layout;
- pane host selection;
- pane surface selection.

It should not implement or understand feature-specific behavior such as Restart, Isolation, Diagnostics, Snapshots, Mods, or server lifecycle semantics.

## Surface validity

Client and server surfaces differ.

If a pane changes to a host for which its currently selected surface is invalid, Ephervex should choose a deterministic valid fallback rather than entering an invalid state.

The same applies if:

- a selected host is removed;
- a surface becomes unavailable;
- a restored layout references stale state.

## Persistence

Persistence of the layout is optional for the first implementation.

If it is implemented, only composition/presentation state should be persisted. Feature-owned runtime or workflow state must not be copied into pane records.

A normal single-pane startup remains a valid default.

## UX

The existing single-workspace experience should remain simple.

Split controls should be discoverable without turning the normal workspace into a complex layout editor.

Equal pane sizing is sufficient for the initial implementation unless a stronger requirement emerges.

## Non-goals

Do not implement:

- arbitrary recursive splits;
- IDE-style docking;
- floating panes;
- detachable windows;
- tab groups;
- arbitrary N-pane layouts;
- layout presets;
- linked client/server behavior;
- automatic pair creation;
- pair startup logic.

Split Workspace is generic composition infrastructure, not the linked workflow itself.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] All supported layouts can be created.
- [ ] Closing panes produces deterministic valid layouts.
- [ ] Each pane independently selects a host.
- [ ] Each pane independently selects a surface.
- [ ] The same host can appear in multiple panes.
- [ ] Different surfaces for the same host remain independently selected.
- [ ] Underlying host/feature state remains shared.
- [ ] Duplicate host operations are still prevented globally per host.
- [ ] Invalid surfaces normalize to a valid fallback.
- [ ] Removing a selected host does not leave an invalid workspace.
- [ ] Existing single-pane behavior remains intact.
- [ ] Split-specific code does not duplicate existing host feature logic.
- [ ] Relevant model/controller/headless UI tests cover the composition boundary.

## Summary

Add an explicit relationship between a client instance and a local server to support Ephervex's developer-oriented Minecraft workflow.

A developer should be able to associate a client with a local server, clearly see which pair is active, navigate both sides easily, and optionally have one side start when the other is launched.

This should build on the generic Split Workspace system rather than introducing a separate hard-coded client/server screen.

## Pair selection

The user should be able to:

- select a client in a user-friendly way;
- select one of the relevant local servers;
- identify the server by useful information such as name and port;
- clearly see which client/server pair is currently associated;
- change the selected pair;
- clear the relationship.

The relationship should not depend on which pane happens to be selected.

## Split Workspace integration

A linked pair should be easy to display side by side using Split Workspace.

However, the relationship itself must remain separate from the layout.

A linked client and server do not have to occupy two panes at all times.

Either side should still be able to display ordinary host surfaces such as:

- Overview;
- Mods;
- Logs;
- Diagnostics;
- Isolation;
- Snapshots;
- server Console/status tools.

Split Workspace must remain generic enough to also display unrelated hosts.

## Start behavior

When pair-start behavior is enabled:

- starting the client may start the linked server;
- starting the server may start the linked client;
- both do not need to start simultaneously;
- an already-running counterpart must not be launched again.

The implementation must prevent feedback loops such as:

`client starts → server starts → client starts again`

Pair operations need enough state to distinguish an intentional counterpart start from a new independent trigger.

## Restart behavior

An intentional restart of either host must not break the relationship or accidentally trigger duplicate counterpart launches.

Existing host restart behavior should remain distinct from fresh start behavior.

The linked workflow should use normal host lifecycle primitives rather than simulating GUI button presses.

Future pair restart functionality should have a deterministic model and must not create client/server restart loops.

## Failure handling

If starting one side succeeds and the counterpart fails:

- the relationship should remain understandable;
- the pair operation must not remain permanently "busy";
- the successfully running host should not automatically be killed unless explicitly defined by the action;
- the failure should clearly identify which side failed.

## Persistence

Decide explicitly whether linked relationships survive an Ephervex restart.

If persisted, stale or deleted host references must be handled safely.

## Future compatibility

The relationship should be designed so later developer workflows can make use of it, particularly linked client/server reproduction or isolation testing.

That later work must still maintain independent dependency graphs for client and server.

## Non-goals

Do not turn this into:

- general process orchestration;
- remote server management;
- arbitrary service dependency graphs;
- hosting-provider integration;
- automatic permanent pairing of every client and server;
- mandatory simultaneous startup.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] A client/server pair can be selected.
- [ ] The active relationship is clearly visible.
- [ ] The pair can be changed or cleared.
- [ ] A pair can be displayed naturally through Split Workspace.
- [ ] Either host can still display any normal valid host surface.
- [ ] Starting one side can optionally start the counterpart.
- [ ] Already-running counterparts are not duplicated.
- [ ] Client→server→client feedback loops are prevented.
- [ ] Intentional restart does not trigger duplicate pair startup.
- [ ] Pair operation state recovers cleanly from partial failure.
- [ ] Deleted/stale pair members are handled safely.

## Summary

Improve Ephervex's local-server workflow for Minecraft development without turning the launcher into a general-purpose server hosting/admin panel.

Local servers are primarily development infrastructure. Ephervex should make the common development operations easy while leaving arbitrary file management and advanced administration to normal system tools.

## Server status

Provide useful, immediately understandable local-server state such as:

- stopped;
- starting;
- running;
- stopping;
- restarting;
- failed/crashed;
- otherwise unavailable or requiring attention where relevant.

The user should not have to infer basic server lifecycle state from logs alone.

## Logs

Make server logs easy to access from the normal host workflow.

Useful behavior includes:

- quick navigation to current/recent server output;
- access to persisted server logs;
- clear distinction between launcher-captured output and files written by Minecraft/server software where relevant;
- easy navigation from a failure/crash to the relevant log.

## Console

Keep console support lightweight.

A local server console is useful when Ephervex directly owns and controls the running server process, but the feature should remain focused on development convenience rather than becoming a complete server panel.

Useful capabilities may include:

- viewing current output;
- sending normal server commands;
- clearly showing when interactive console access is unavailable.

Do not pretend console access can be recovered when Ephervex no longer owns a valid live control channel.

## File/configuration access

`Open Folder` should remain the primary mechanism for arbitrary server files.

Useful shortcuts may include:

- Open Server Folder;
- open relevant configuration location;
- open logs;
- open crash reports;
- navigate to common Ephervex-managed server data.

Avoid building an embedded general-purpose file manager.

## Integration

Server tooling should use the same host/workspace model as other Ephervex functionality.

It should work naturally when displayed:

- in the ordinary single workspace;
- inside Split Workspace;
- as one side of a linked client/server pair.

## Non-goals

Do not implement:

- remote server administration;
- player management dashboards;
- permission/role management;
- hosting-provider integrations;
- remote file management;
- web-panel replacement;
- arbitrary server orchestration;
- backup systems unrelated to Ephervex snapshots.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Local-server lifecycle state is obvious.
- [ ] Relevant server logs are easy to reach.
- [ ] Open Server Folder is easy to access.
- [ ] Console functionality remains lightweight and honest about availability.
- [ ] Existing start/stop/restart behavior integrates consistently.
- [ ] Server tooling works inside normal HostWorkspace composition.
- [ ] The feature does not expand into general hosting administration.

## Summary

Polish the common developer workflows that currently require unnecessary navigation or make important host state harder to see than it should be.

This issue is intentionally about small, high-value workflow improvements rather than introducing another major subsystem.

## Folder access

Provide convenient actions for:

- Open Instance Folder;
- Open Server Folder.

These actions should be available from logical places in the host workspace without forcing the user to search through Settings or unrelated menus.

## Logs and crash information

Make relevant logs and crash reports easy to reach.

A developer should be able to move quickly from:

- a running host;
- a failed launch;
- a crash notice;
- Diagnostics;

to the logs or crash report relevant to that host.

## Running state

Make it obvious whether a client/server is:

- stopped;
- running;
- starting/stopping;
- restarting;
- otherwise in an operation state.

This should remain consistent across different places where the same host appears, including Split Workspace.

## Linked state

Once linked client/server workflow exists, clearly indicate:

- whether a host is linked;
- which counterpart it is linked to;
- whether the counterpart is currently running.

The linked relationship should be visible without overwhelming the normal host UI.

## Tested configuration

When Ephervex temporarily changes a host configuration for a developer workflow, make it clear which configuration is currently active.

This is especially important for workflows such as mod isolation/reproduction where the currently enabled mod set may intentionally differ from the user's normal configuration.

The UI should avoid leaving the developer uncertain whether they are looking at:

- the original configuration;
- an experimental configuration;
- a restored configuration;
- an incomplete/recovery state.

## UX principles

Prefer:

- direct navigation;
- small contextual actions;
- clear state labels;
- consistent HostWorkspace behavior.

Avoid:

- duplicating the same status in many unrelated places;
- large new dashboards;
- hidden state that requires opening another page to understand.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Open Instance Folder is easy to access.
- [ ] Open Server Folder is easy to access.
- [ ] Relevant logs are reachable with minimal navigation.
- [ ] Crash reports are easy to reach when one exists.
- [ ] Running/lifecycle state is consistently visible.
- [ ] Linked state is clearly visible where relevant.
- [ ] Temporary/test configuration state is clear during developer workflows.
- [ ] Improvements remain consistent in single and split workspaces.

## Summary

Move Ephervex's embedded Slint UI definitions out of `crates/gui/src/ui.rs` and into normal standalone `.slint` files/modules.

The current approach works, but the UI has grown large enough that keeping the Slint DSL embedded inside Rust is becoming inconvenient to maintain.

`crates/gui/src/ui.rs` is currently roughly 6,200+ lines long, much of which is declarative Slint UI rather than Rust application logic.

Using real `.slint` files should improve:

- code organization;
- separation between UI markup and Rust glue;
- IDE support;
- Slint syntax highlighting;
- completion/navigation through the Slint IDE plugin;
- readability of UI changes;
- reviewability of future GUI work.

## Goal

Keep Rust responsible for:

- controller logic;
- backend communication;
- presentation/state conversion;
- callback wiring;
- application behavior.

Move declarative Slint definitions into dedicated `.slint` modules.

The resulting Rust `ui` module should mostly expose/import the generated Slint types needed by the rest of the GUI crate instead of containing thousands of lines of embedded UI markup.

## Suggested structure

The exact structure should follow natural component boundaries rather than splitting files solely by line count.

A possible layout could be:

    crates/gui/
    ├── src/
    │   ├── ui.rs
    │   ├── controller.rs
    │   ├── backend.rs
    │   └── presentation.rs
    └── ui/
        ├── app.slint
        ├── theme.slint
        ├── components/
        │   ├── buttons.slint
        │   ├── cards.slint
        │   ├── dialogs.slint
        │   └── ...
        ├── workspace/
        │   ├── host-workspace.slint
        │   ├── overview.slint
        │   ├── mods.slint
        │   ├── isolation.slint
        │   ├── diagnostics.slint
        │   ├── snapshots.slint
        │   └── logs.slint
        └── settings/
            └── ...

This is only an example. Avoid creating dozens of tiny files if larger cohesive modules are easier to understand.

## Preserve the Rust-facing API

Existing Rust code and tests currently use generated Slint types such as:

- `AppWindow`;
- `GuiI18n`;
- row structs/models;
- callbacks;
- properties;
- globals.

Where practical, preserve the existing Rust-facing API so this remains primarily a source-layout refactor rather than a broad controller/backend rewrite.

Avoid renaming public/generated types unless there is a clear reason.

## Component boundaries

Use the migration as an opportunity to separate large visual areas into understandable modules, especially components that already have clear responsibilities such as:

- theme/shared primitives;
- application shell;
- HostWorkspace;
- Mods;
- Isolation;
- Diagnostics;
- Snapshots;
- Logs;
- Settings;
- dialogs/modals;
- reusable rows/cards/buttons.

Do not move application or feature ownership into Slint merely because the markup is being reorganized.

The existing architectural principle should remain:

- Rust owns application/feature state;
- Slint renders that state and emits user actions.

## Build integration

Use the normal Slint build/module mechanism for standalone `.slint` sources.

Ensure:

- Cargo builds work from a clean checkout;
- required `.slint` files are included correctly;
- asset paths continue to resolve;
- generated Rust bindings remain available to the GUI crate;
- packaging/release builds do not depend on IDE-specific behavior.

Avoid introducing generated files into Git unless the existing build conventions require them.

## IDE support

One of the explicit goals is to make the UI directly understandable by Slint-aware IDE tooling.

After the migration, RustRover/IntelliJ with the Slint plugin should be able to recognize the `.slint` sources and provide normal language support where available.

## Incremental migration

This does not need to be performed as one giant mechanical move.

Prefer logical migration steps where each commit still builds, for example:

1. establish standalone Slint build/import infrastructure;
2. move shared theme/primitives;
3. move reusable components;
4. move HostWorkspace and host surfaces;
5. move application shell/settings/modals;
6. reduce `ui.rs` to the Rust-facing integration layer.

The final branch should not leave duplicate embedded and standalone implementations behind.

## Testing

This is primarily a refactor, so existing behavior should remain unchanged.

Run existing GUI/headless tests throughout the migration.

Pay particular attention to tests using:

- `AppWindow`;
- generated row types;
- globals;
- callback invocation;
- accessible labels;
- headless rendering;
- Slint testing backend.

Add focused tests only where the migration changes integration/build boundaries.

## Non-goals

Do not use this issue to:

- redesign the GUI;
- change application behavior;
- redesign controller/backend ownership;
- implement Split Workspace;
- rewrite localization;
- replace Slint;
- introduce a new state-management architecture.

Small cleanup needed to make module boundaries work is fine, but feature changes should remain separate issues.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Slint UI definitions live in standalone `.slint` files.
- [ ] `crates/gui/src/ui.rs` is substantially reduced.
- [ ] Major UI areas have understandable module boundaries.
- [ ] Existing Rust-facing generated types remain usable.
- [ ] Existing controller/backend behavior is unchanged.
- [ ] Existing GUI/headless tests continue to pass.
- [ ] Clean builds work without IDE-specific setup.
- [ ] Assets and localization continue to resolve correctly.
- [ ] The Slint IDE plugin can recognize the UI sources as `.slint` files.
- [ ] No unrelated feature work is bundled into the migration.

## Summary

Improve common local-server configuration tasks while keeping Ephervex focused on local development infrastructure.

This should extend the P1 local-server workflow rather than turning Ephervex into a hosting control panel.

## Server status and identity

Present useful server information together, including where available:

- lifecycle state;
- Minecraft version;
- loader/version;
- local address/port;
- linked client relationship.

The user should be able to identify the server and its current state without reading logs.

## Logs and output

Improve access to:

- live/current output when Ephervex owns a valid control channel;
- persisted server logs;
- crash reports;
- failure output.

If live console access is unavailable because the process is orphaned or no longer controllable, say so explicitly rather than presenting a non-functional console.

## Files and configuration

Keep **Open Server Folder** as the primary mechanism for arbitrary file management.

Provide useful direct navigation to:

- server configuration;
- logs;
- crash reports;
- other relevant server-owned locations.

## Focused configuration controls

Expose common server settings through dedicated controls only where Ephervex understands their semantics and can round-trip them safely.

Requirements:

- validate typed values such as ports where applicable;
- preserve unknown/unrelated configuration;
- do not rewrite or discard settings Ephervex does not own;
- if safe editing cannot be guaranteed, prefer opening the actual configuration file externally.

## Restart-required changes

When a setting only takes effect after restart, make that clear.

Saving configuration must not implicitly restart the server.

Restart remains a separate explicit lifecycle action.

## Lightweight console

Where Ephervex has a live valid control channel, allow basic server commands.

The console should remain a development convenience, not a full hosting console/dashboard.

## Split and linked workflow integration

The server remains a normal HostWorkspace target.

Server configuration conveniences should work naturally when the host is:

- shown normally;
- displayed in Split Workspace;
- part of a linked client/server pair.

Other normal server surfaces such as Logs, Diagnostics, Mods, Isolation, and Overview remain independently selectable.

## Safety

Configuration changes should respect the same host-operation/mutation safety rules used elsewhere.

Do not mutate configuration concurrently with incompatible host operations.

## Non-goals

Do not implement:

- remote server administration;
- player dashboards;
- permission/role management;
- hosting-provider integrations;
- remote file management;
- web-panel replacement;
- arbitrary service orchestration.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Local-server status/identity are clear.
- [ ] Logs and crash output are easy to reach.
- [ ] Open Server Folder remains the primary arbitrary-file action.
- [ ] Common configuration can be edited only where safe semantics are known.
- [ ] Unknown/unrelated configuration is preserved.
- [ ] Restart-required settings clearly communicate that requirement.
- [ ] Saving configuration does not implicitly restart.
- [ ] Lightweight console input is available only when a valid control channel exists.
- [ ] The feature works naturally inside Split Workspace and linked workflows.
- [ ] The feature does not expand into general hosting administration.

## Summary

Reduce navigation friction and uncertainty during repeated development, debugging, and relaunch cycles.

This is a collection of small, high-value workflow refinements built on the P1 host workspace, Split Workspace, linked client/server workflow, and Diagnostics foundations.

It should not become a new developer dashboard.

## Folder shortcuts

Make the following easy to reach from the active host workspace:

- **Open Instance Folder** for clients;
- **Open Server Folder** for local servers.

The user should not need to navigate through unrelated settings to reach the host they are already viewing.

## Logs and crash reports

Provide direct/contextual navigation to relevant evidence from:

- a running host;
- a failed client launch/server start;
- a crash notice;
- Diagnostics.

The destination should remain scoped to the host the user was acting on.

## Lifecycle visibility

Keep host state obvious wherever that host is displayed.

Represent meaningful states such as:

- starting;
- running;
- stopping;
- restarting;
- stopped;
- failed;
- orphaned/attention states where applicable.

If the same host is visible in multiple Split Workspace panes, all panes should reflect the same underlying lifecycle state.

## Linked state

Once a client/server relationship exists, clearly show:

- whether the current host is linked;
- which counterpart it is linked to;
- whether the counterpart is currently running.

The indication should be useful but compact; normal host surfaces should not become pair-management screens.

## Active test configuration

Clearly indicate which configuration is currently under test.

This is particularly important for Isolation/reproduction workflows where the active mod set may intentionally differ from the host's normal configuration.

Distinguish states such as:

- normal/canonical configuration;
- active temporary test configuration;
- restore/recovery in progress;
- other temporary developer-test state introduced later.

The user should not have to guess whether they are testing the original configuration or a temporary one.

## Cross-surface context

Preserve useful host context when moving among:

- Overview;
- Mods;
- Logs;
- Diagnostics;
- Isolation;
- Snapshots;
- server-specific surfaces.

Avoid unnecessary host reselection and ensure contextual actions always target the host actually being viewed.

## Contextual debugging navigation

Where useful, provide direct routes such as:

- launch/start failure -> Diagnostics or relevant logs;
- crash -> crash report/logs/Diagnostics;
- active workflow -> its owning surface.

These should be navigation conveniences, not duplicated feature implementations.

## Split Workspace consistency

Pane selection is presentation state only.

Do not create pane-local copies of:

- lifecycle state;
- linked relationship;
- workflow state;
- attention/diagnostic state.

The same host shown twice should remain visibly consistent.

## Non-goals

Do not:

- add a new all-in-one developer dashboard;
- duplicate feature state per pane;
- add unrelated workflow automation;
- hide host identity behind global selection state.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Open Instance Folder is easy to reach.
- [ ] Open Server Folder is easy to reach.
- [ ] Relevant logs/crash reports are reachable contextually.
- [ ] Lifecycle state is consistent wherever a host is displayed.
- [ ] Linked counterpart/state are clearly visible where relevant.
- [ ] Active temporary/test configuration is clearly distinguished from normal state.
- [ ] Navigation preserves the intended host context.
- [ ] Failed launch/start and crash flows can reach relevant Diagnostics/logs quickly.
- [ ] Split Workspace panes share host-owned state rather than duplicating it.
- [ ] The result remains a set of focused workflow improvements, not a new dashboard.

